sentences.l:
But when they had come among the Trojans and the Achaeans,
from the horses having stepped down onto the much-nourishing earth,
into the middle of the Trojans and Achaeans they were marching.

And Atreus’-son, drawing with his hands the knife
that always hung beside the great sheath of his sword,
from the heads of the lambs cut hairs;

If Alexander should kill Menelaus,
let him himself then have Helen and all the possessions,
and let us go back in our sea-crossing ships;

For my part, I am going to windy Ilios
back, since by no means shall I endure to look with my eyes
upon my dear son fighting with Ares-loved Menelaus;

And they, when now on either side of the throng they had armed themselves,
into the middle of the Trojans and Achaeans were marching,
looking dreadfully;
